 {"id": "36c094ca5b33", "title": "Deduplicate Telegram bot subscribe/keyboard logic in telegram_bot.py", "description": "`app/core/telegram_bot.py` has significant duplication:\n\n1. `subscribe_to_endpoint_callback` (lines ~325-409) and `subscribe_to_endpoint` (lines ~411-483) implement almost the same subscribe-or-activate logic against two different Update surfaces (callback query vs message). The only real difference is which reply method is called and how the endpoint is fetched.\n2. `subscribe_command` (~150-196) and `refresh_endpoints_callback` (~525-566) both build an identical inline keyboard from the endpoint list and post the identical message text.\n3. `handler_command` for `/unsubscribe` is currently wired to `list_subscriptions_command` \u2014 probably a copy/paste bug but confirm before changing behavior; if intentional, add a short comment noting so.\n\n## What to change (ONLY this file)\n1. Extract `_upsert_subscription(cur, endpoint_id: int, chat_id: str) -> tuple[str, str]` returning `(status, endpoint_name)` where status is one of `\"already\"`, `\"reactivated\"`, `\"created\"`. Both `subscribe_to_endpoint_callback` and `subscribe_to_endpoint` should call it and pick the right reply text from a small dict.\n2. Extract `_build_endpoint_keyboard(endpoints: list[dict]) -> InlineKeyboardMarkup` and `_ENDPOINT_LIST_TEXT: str` module constants; use them in both `subscribe_command` and `refresh_endpoints_callback`.\n3. For the `/unsubscribe` handler wiring: if it is a bug (users expect unsubscribe, not list), rewire to a new small handler that shows only unsubscribe buttons. If preserving old behavior, add a `# NOTE: /unsubscribe intentionally reuses /list to show the unsubscribe buttons inline.` comment on that line.\n4. Preserve every user-facing message string exactly. No emoji or wording drift.\n5. Do not touch the DB schema, other modules, or add tests (Task 1 owns tests).\n\n## Files you own\n`app/core/telegram_bot.py` \u2014 nothing else.\n\n## Ac